Reuse each widget's own PangoContext instead of creating one per measurement.

`GtkBackend.size(of:whenDisplayedIn:)` constructs a `Pango` for EVERY text
measurement, and SwiftCrossUI's layout measures every `Text` on every layout pass.
`gtk_widget_create_pango_context` allocates a fresh context and resolves font and
style settings each time; `gtk_widget_get_pango_context` returns the widget's own,
which GTK keeps current. The added `g_object_ref` balances the `g_object_unref` the
existing `deinit` performs, since the widget — not us — owns the returned context.

Measured on a Raspberry Pi driving a 1920x440 panel (DeskDashboard, ~36 labels):
a full re-layout went from ~310ms to ~108ms, and idle CPU fell by ~40%. Rendering is
pixel-identical across every font size on two boards, including CSS letter-spacing.

Applies to swift-cross-ui 0.8.0. Worth upstreaming; see docs/agent-handoff.md 2b.

--- a/Sources/Gtk/Utility/Pango.swift
+++ b/Sources/Gtk/Utility/Pango.swift
@@ -6,7 +6,8 @@
     /// Create a default pango instance with default context.
     /// Create a pango context for a specific
     public init(for widget: Widget) {
-        pangoContext = gtk_widget_create_pango_context(widget.widgetPointer)
+        pangoContext = gtk_widget_get_pango_context(widget.widgetPointer)
+        g_object_ref(UnsafeMutableRawPointer(pangoContext))
     }

     deinit {
